{"sob":{"id":"02ddc4a6-1ea2-4b3f-a5bb-7f8a845c66e1","word":"sob","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"weep with convulsive gasps","word":"soluçar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"She was sobbing because she was feeling very miserable.","bold_text_offsets":[[8,15]],"type":"example","id":"6cdb1809-562a-4b52-a392-342643d2b44c"},{"text":"She sigh'd, she sobb'd, and, furious with despair, / She rent her garments, and she tore her hair.","ref":"1697, Virgil, “Pastoral 5”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[16,22]],"id":"d4df1508-ff76-4730-81de-dae4d3fb1e01"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["weep","weep"],["convulsive","convulsive"],["gasp","gasp"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To weep with convulsive gasps."],"glosses":["To weep with convulsive gasps."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"acb4f204-3015-42c4-96fb-99c760858687"},{"examples":[{"text":"\"He doesn't love me!\" she sobbed.","bold_text_offsets":[[26,32]],"type":"example","id":"762ec2c2-5414-4162-b9c5-83743115f322"}],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To say (something) while sobbing."],"glosses":["To say (something) while sobbing."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"b818f125-bb23-4e63-bf03-910b76a92d0c"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"sobriquet":{"id":"595622a4-98df-42f2-a800-4bd2ebda6f19","word":"sobriquet","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"familiar name for a person or thing","tags":["feminine"],"word":"alcunha"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"“The Bard” is a sobriquet of English playwright William Shakespeare.","bold_text_offsets":[[16,25]],"type":"example","id":"7e0ca933-6113-460d-8828-c83d4ea2ecf1"},{"text":"So began the memorable paper-chase for an extremely nebulous shop-assistant and a foreigner whose description began and ended with the sobriquet 'Peter the Italian'.","ref":"1927, Ernest Bramah, Max Carrados Mysteries:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[135,144]],"id":"16025d5b-b676-4631-a6c6-84aa35bb44c7"},{"text":"They turned out to be speedy machines and earned the sobriquet \"Greyhounds\", giving excellent performances on the Exeter run.","ref":"1951 November, David R. Webb, “The Drummond 4-4-0 Locomotives”, in Railway Magazine, page 774:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[53,62]],"id":"48c0fc37-0d2e-4399-8737-170d4ea7a4cf"},{"text":"The sobriquet of Johnny Appleseed attached to him, though his real name was Chapman, in consequence of his being ever engaged in gathering and planting appleseed and cultivating nurseries of apple trees.","ref":"1862, A. Banning Norton, (Please provide the book title or journal name):","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,13]],"id":"01615568-47bd-4b24-b57a-eac0d7709a5a"},{"text":"Tight quickly became a Jumeirah Jane—a sobriquet used as a caricature for non-working foreign women who live in Jumeirah, a posh part of the city, and spent their time driving kids to and from schools, shopping or meeting one another for lunch.","ref":"2011, Pranay Gupte, Dubai: The Making of a Megapolis, Penguin Books India, →ISBN, page 344:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[39,48]],"id":"7ad3be36-958b-477e-bcc9-b0afc116a2b0"}],"links":[["familiar","familiar"],["nickname","nickname"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"cognomen"},{"word":"moniker"},{"word":"nickname"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms borrowed from French","English terms derived from French","English terms derived from Middle French","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 2 entries","Pages wit